Lab 9: Cloud Security – Visual Firewall Monitoring
Objective
Capture network traffic using Wireshark, convert the data into structured format (e.g., CSV), and use Python visualization and computer vision techniques to highlight potential security threats — all offline on your Ubuntu system.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine
Install tools:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install wireshark tshark -y
pip install pandas matplotlib opencv-python
During Wireshark setup, if prompted, allow non-root users to capture packets:

sudo usermod -aG wireshark $USER
newgrp wireshark
Steps
Step 1: Capture Network Traffic with Wireshark or tshark
Start Wireshark GUI:
wireshark
Or use tshark CLI for faster, scriptable capture:
tshark -i any -a duration:30 -w capture.pcapng
This captures 30 seconds of traffic and saves it to capture.pcapng.

Step 2: Convert pcap to CSV
Use tshark to extract essential packet data:

tshark -r capture.pcapng -T fields -e frame.number -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e frame.len -e _ws.col.Protocol -E header=y -E separator=, -E quote=d -E occurrence=f > traffic.csv
Now you have a structured traffic.csv with key fields.

Step 3: Visualize Network Traffic
Create a Python script visualize_traffic.py:
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

df = pd.read_csv("traffic.csv")

# Drop rows with missing IPs
df = df.dropna(subset=["ip.src", "ip.dst"])

# Count number of packets per source IP
top_sources = df['ip.src'].value_counts().head(10)

plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
top_sources.plot(kind='bar', color='skyblue')
plt.title("Top 10 Source IPs by Packet Count")
plt.xlabel("Source IP")
plt.ylabel("Packets")
plt.tight_layout()
plt.savefig("traffic_bar_chart.png")
plt.show()
Run the script:
python3 visualize_traffic.py
You’ll get a bar chart highlighting the busiest source IPs.

Step 4: Highlight Suspicious Activity (e.g., Flooding)
Extend the script to flag possible anomalies:

suspicious = top_sources[top_sources > 100]
if not suspicious.empty:
    print("\n⚠️  Potential Threats Detected:")
    print(suspicious)
else:
    print("\n✅  No suspicious IPs found.")
You can also use OpenCV for custom rendering of heatmaps or visual overlays if desired.

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Captured real network traffic with Wireshark or tshark

Converted .pcapng to structured CSV format

Visualized network behavior and flagged abnormal patterns using Python

Simulated firewall-level anomaly detection — entirely offline

This lab gives you a strong foundation in using visual tools to monitor and secure network activity in real-time — without needing cloud services.
